Chain-derived order: parse `implication_ledger.extension_class_containment`. The canonical text: "E_C0 contains E_H2loc contains E_{C^1,1} contains E_C2; ...". I'll extract tokens in order of appearance: E_C0, E_H2loc, E_{C^1,1}, E_C2 → set order largest→smallest. Entailment order (strongest statement → weakest): C0, H2LOC, C11, C2 (smallest set = strongest inextendibility). So for a class at position i in entailment order, correct direction: its conclusion entails the class at position i+1 (the next weaker). E.g., C0 ⇒ H2LOC; H2LOC ⇒ C2 (C11 skipped in texts).

Class token normalization: class_id AF-SCC-C0-VAC-GEN → C0; AF-SCC-C2-VAC-GEN → C2. For this class in the chain, map token: C0→E_C0, C2→E_C2. The "next weaker" from chain order.

Classifier:
```
def classify(text, this_tok, ent_order):
    # strip bracketed correction notes
    stripped = remove_brackets(text)  # remove [ ... ] segments
    live_denial = bool(re.search(r'no\s+containment\s+with', stripped, re.I))
    # find entailment assertions
    ent_claims = []
    # pattern A: "<X>-inextendibility ... entails ... this class"  -> X entails this
    # pattern B: "this class'?s? ... entails ... <X>" / "C0-inextendibility is the strongest ... ENTAILS"
    # Generic: split into sentences; for each sentence containing 'entail', find left subject and right object.
```
Simpler robust approach: check explicit directional phrases:
- inverted_phrase = re.search(r'(H2_loc|C2|C0)[- ]inextendibility\s+ENTAILS\s+this class', s, re.I) → subject X entails this class. Inverted if X is weaker than this class (i.e., position of X > position of this in ent_order).
- correct_phrase = re.search(r'this class(?:\'s)?[^.]*?(?:conclusion|inextendibility)[^.]*?ENTAILS\s+(?:the\s+)?(H2_loc|C2|C0)', s, re.I) OR re.search(r'(C0|C2|H2_loc)-inextendibility is the strongest[^.]*ENTAILS', s, re.I).
Let me just implement a small entailment extractor:
```
ENT_RE = re.compile(r'([A-Za-z0-9_^{\}\'\s]{0,60}?)(?:-|\s)inextendibility\s+(?:is[^.]*?)?(?:therefore\s+|thus\s+|so\s+)?ENTAILS\s+([A-Za-z0-9_^{\}\'\s]{0,60})', re.I)
```
That's fragile. Given candidate texts are known and few, I can use targeted patterns:
- P_INV = r'(H2_loc|C2|C0)-inextendibility\s+ENTAILS\s+this class' (case-insens; also "so H2_loc-inextendibility ENTAILS this class's conclusion")
- P_CORR_A = r"this class'?s?\s+(?:C0-)?inextendibility[^.]{0,80}?ENTAILS[^.]{0,40}?(H2_loc|C2)"
- P_CORR_B = r"(C0|C2)-inextendibility is the strongest[^.]{0,80}?ENTAILS"
- P_SIB = r'entails the C2 sibling' (direction info for H2loc ⇒ C2)
- P_AGNOSTIC = r'direction of the induced entailments[^.]*recorded there' or no entail verb in the H2 sentence.

Then determine expected direction for this class:
- If match P_INV and subject_position > this_position (subject is weaker) → INVERTED_ENTAILMENT.
- If P_CORR matches and target position = this_position+1 → CORRECT_ENTAILMENT.
